://twitter.com/TaiwanNewsEN &quot;X&quot;)[![Youtube](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/assets/footer-youtube.svg)](https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCuLruQX4dTXn89WS9A3ccJg &quot;Youtube&quot;)[![IG](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/assets/footer-ig.svg)](https://www.instagram.com/taiwannews_/ &quot;IG&quot;)\n\nTaiwan News © 2026 All Rights Reserved.\n\n[# 2026 local elections](&lt;https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/topic/2026 local elections&gt;)\n\n[Home](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en)\n\n/\n\n[Politics](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/category/Politics)\n\n# US-China war over Taiwan could cost world NT$332.55 trillion\n\nBloomberg models dire economic fallout from cross-strait conflict\n\n Feb. 12, 2026 15:45\n\n![Flags of Taiwan and the US. (Reuters, Stephen Lam photo)](https://image.taiwannews.com.tw/2026%2F02%2F12%2F342b76d2fd59452b8521cdda0fb7fa61.jpeg &quot;Flags of Taiwan and the US. (Reuters, Stephen Lam photo)&quot;)\n\nFlags of Taiwan and the US. (Reuters, Stephen Lam photo)\n\n16416\n\nTAIPEI (Taiwan News) — A war between the US and China over Taiwan could inflict NT$332.55 trillion (US$10.6 trillion) in losses in the first year alone and erase 9.6% of global GDP, according to [Bloomberg Economics](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-02-10/the-10-trillion-fight-modeling-a-us-china-war-over-taiwan).\n\nThe media outlet said Tuesday the damage would exceed the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic or the global financial crisis in 2008.\n\nIt said Taiwan sits at the heart of the vulnerability, producing 62% of the world’s most advanced semiconductors that power everything from AI data centers and smartphones to cars and aircraft. TSMC alone generates about 70% of global foundry revenue, supplying firms such as Nvidia, Apple, and AMD.\n\nBloomberg outlined five scenarios ranging from a full-scale Chinese invasion that draws in US forces to an unlikely rapprochement between Taipei and Beijing. The war case envisions a collapse in US-China trade, 50% tariffs imposed by US allies on China, and severe disruptions to shipping in the Taiwan Strait.\n\nBloomberg projected Taiwan’s economy would shrink 40% due to direct damage in a war, based on recent conflicts. It forecast an 11% contraction for China and a 6.6% hit to the US, driven by chip shortages and supply chain disruptions for companies such as Apple and Nvidia.\n\nBloomberg estimated South Korea’s GDP could fall 23%, Japan’s 14.7%, the European Union’s 10.9%, and India’s 8%. It attributed the decline to semiconductor scarcity, trade sanctions, and disrupted maritime flows through the Taiwan Strait, a route it said handles more than one-fifth of global shipping.\n\nBloomberg described President Lai Ching-te (賴清德) as championing Taiwan’s distinct democratic identity while Chinese leader Xi Jinping grows more impatient for annexation. Meanwhile, China’s military modernization is said to have eroded the US’ once-decisive edge, tilting geography and logistics closer to Beijing’s favor.\n\nEven a blockade short of invasion would cut global GDP 5.3% and Taiwan’s 12.5%, Bloomberg projected, as chip inventories dwindle within weeks and production halts in dependent industries. Heightened tensions without a full crisis could trim global output 0.3% through modest tariffs and trade frictions, marking the most probable near-term path amid escalating gray-zone tactics from China.\n\nWhile all-out war remains unlikely for now — given invasion risks to China’s untested forces and uncertainty over US intervention — Bloomberg’s modeling underscored Taiwan’s dual role as a linchpin of global chipmaking and a geopolitical fault line.\n\n[Support Us](https://taiwannews.oen.tw/)\n\n[# global economy](&lt;https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/topic/global economy&gt;)[# semiconductors](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/topic/semiconductors)[# Taiwan Strait](&lt;https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/topic/Taiwan Strait&gt;)\n\n## Related Articles\n\n[Australia, Germany reaffirm support for peace in Taiwan Strait Feb. 9, 2026 13:58](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6298905)\n\n[Taiwan tracks 1 Chinese balloon and 7 naval vessels Feb. 8, 2026 11:55](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6298513)\n\n[French diplomat defends Taiwan Strait missions as upholding international law Feb. 5, 2026 17:26](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6296981)\n\n[Japan representative underscores importance of partnership with Taiwan Feb. 2, 2026 15:06](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6294212)\n\n[Taiwan tracks 1 PLA helicopter and 6 naval vessels Feb. 1, 2026 13:29](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6293847)\n\n[Taiwan foreign minister calls for semiconductor cooperation with Indonesia Jan. 27, 2026 17:13](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6290516)\n\nMOST READ\n\n---\n\n[Taipei 101 lights up in support of Jensen Huang after Nvidia exit](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6477097)\n\n[Taiwan’s drone supply chain must adapt to new cross-border controls](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6476829)\n\n[KMT pushes NT$20,000 cash handout ahead of Taiwan local elections](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6477001)\n\n[Taiwan detects 2 Russian military aircraft in ADIZ](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6477302)\n\n[Foreign ministry says Taiwan closely watching Trump’s Asia trip](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6477371)\n\n[Taiwan&#x27;s defense no longer hinges on US carriers](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6476844)\n\n[Taiwan ranks 13th in IMD&#x27;s 2026 global digital and AI competitiveness](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6477203)\n\n[Taiwan in ‘close contact’ with US over historic US-China-Russia meeting](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6478193)\n\n[Officials list 5 ways CCP mobilizes Taiwanese expats in China for Taiwan elections](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6477268)\n\n[Nvidia sought leniency from Taiwan for employee accused of China chip smuggling](https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/6476759)\n\n16416\" da